// `m[i>j]$~ v` as a statement writes into `m` where it lives (GLB-140,
// 2026-10-19): the tree-walker read the receiver as a second owner and copied
// the outer collection on every edit. What the new path must keep: a name that
// shares the value does not see the edit, a row shared by two places is copied
// once and only for the place written, a key is added at the last step, a text
// at the last step is still written, and a refused edit leaves the variable as
// it was. Left to right: an operand that writes the receiver is overwritten
// by the edit of what was read before it (GLB-115).

// A name that shares the value keeps it.
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
u = m
m[1>2]$~ 20
>> m " " u ¶

// A row read out of it keeps what it had, edited once or twice.
r = m[2]
m[2>1]$~ 30
m[2>1]$~ 31
>> r " " m ¶

// A row shared by two places.
fila = [0, 0]
p = [fila, fila]
p[1>1]$~ 5
>> p " " fila ¶

// Three levels, negative positions, a tuple on the way and at the end.
cubo = [[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]]
cubo[-1>-1>-1]$~ 80
cubo[1>2>1]$~ 30
>> cubo ¶
pares = [(1, 2), (3, 4)]
pares[2>1]$~ 33
>> pares ¶

// A dictionary on the way: a key that is there, and one that is added.
d = #(a: #(b: 1), lista: [10, 20])
d.a["b"]$~ 2
d.a["c"]$~ 3
k = "lista"
d[k>2]$~ 200
d.lista$+ 30
>> d ¶

// A text at the last step.
nombres = ["hola", "mundo"]
nombres[2>1]$~ 'M'
>> nombres ¶

// Refused: the variable is as it was after each.
!? { m[9>1]$~ 0 } :! { >> "E1 " _err ¶ }
!? { m[1>0]$~ 0 } :! { >> "E2 " _err ¶ }
!? { m[1>3]$~ 0 } :! { >> "E3 " _err ¶ }
!? { d["zz">1]$~ 0 } :! { >> "E4 " _err ¶ }
!? { d[1>1]$~ 0 } :! { >> "E5 " _err ¶ }
!? { nombres[1>1>1]$~ 'x' } :! { >> "E6 " _err ¶ }
>> m " " d ¶

// An operand that writes the receiver.
crece(v<~) {
    v$+ [100, 100]
    <~ 1
}
w = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
w[(crece(w<~))>2]$~ 0
>> w ¶
